WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:00.875
OK, we're live.

00:00:00.959 --> 00:00:02.919
You are in for an experience.

00:00:04.713 --> 00:00:05.588
Hi, everyone.

00:00:05.588 --> 00:00:06.339
Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:06.840
Welcome.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.008
Thank you for joining us.

00:00:08.008 --> 00:00:09.300
I'm really glad to be part of--

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.094
--this evening's special Q&amp;A.

00:00:11.094 --> 00:00:12.137
--of Boys State.

00:00:12.137 --> 00:00:12.971
Boys State.

00:00:12.971 --> 00:00:13.513
Boys State.

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:15.682
It's a remarkable documentary.

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:18.727
It won the US Grand Jury prize
at the 2020 Sundance Film

00:00:18.727 --> 00:00:19.269
Festival.

00:00:19.269 --> 00:00:20.812
It's an incredible film.

00:00:20.812 --> 00:00:22.105
A riveting documentary.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:23.314
I thought it was fascinating.

00:00:23.314 --> 00:00:23.940
I loved it.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.066
I was weeping by the end.

00:00:25.066 --> 00:00:28.903
This film is fantastic.

00:00:28.903 --> 00:00:32.532
Each year, hundreds of students
gather for a 10 day retreat

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:34.784
to learn about
electoral politics

00:00:34.784 --> 00:00:37.203
at the local and state level.

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:41.041
This is a place where people
from different points of view,

00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:44.044
and different political leanings
get together and talk politics.

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:45.503
The Boys State
program could really

00:00:45.503 --> 00:00:48.256
show us what the country
was feeling and expressing,

00:00:48.256 --> 00:00:51.384
the health of our democracy, and
how young people will hopefully

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:52.343
help lead us forward.

00:00:52.343 --> 00:00:54.471
After seeing the film,
I'm really excited.

00:00:54.471 --> 00:00:55.305
I'm so excited.

00:00:55.305 --> 00:00:55.972
I'm so thrilled.

00:00:55.972 --> 00:00:58.933
--to bring on Amanda
McBaine and Jesse

00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:01.853
Moss-- the filmmakers
behind Boys State

00:01:01.853 --> 00:01:06.191
--and also Robert MacDougall,
Stephen Garza, Rene Otero,

00:01:06.191 --> 00:01:07.942
and Ben Feinstein.

00:01:07.942 --> 00:01:10.737
What do you think you learned
at Boys State about how

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:13.198
politics actually works?

00:01:13.198 --> 00:01:13.907
Politics is hard.

00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:14.824
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:14.824 --> 00:01:17.285
People in this country are
starved for good leadership.

00:01:17.285 --> 00:01:20.038
If you look at the other
side only as the enemy,

00:01:20.038 --> 00:01:22.832
it doesn't leave any room
to actually work together.

00:01:22.832 --> 00:01:25.919
This is such a wild moment for
this movie to be coming out.

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:29.005
Young people engaging in
conversation and debate,

00:01:29.005 --> 00:01:31.841
during a moment when our
democracy needs this.

00:01:31.841 --> 00:01:32.967
It's so timely.

00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:35.845
It really does hold up a
mirror to the divisions

00:01:35.845 --> 00:01:37.806
that we face as a country.

00:01:37.806 --> 00:01:40.683
Get yourselves ready for
a turbulent election.

00:01:40.683 --> 00:01:42.310
What are some of the
themes that you're

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:45.313
hoping arise, from the film,
that perhaps can inform us

00:01:45.313 --> 00:01:47.357
to a better state of politics?

00:01:47.357 --> 00:01:49.651
Most people, in the
end, want what's

00:01:49.651 --> 00:01:51.361
best for their state
and their country,

00:01:51.361 --> 00:01:53.196
and the only difference
is in the ways

00:01:53.196 --> 00:01:54.239
we have of getting there.

00:01:54.239 --> 00:01:56.157
We might have different
political views,

00:01:56.157 --> 00:01:58.409
we might come from different
cities or backgrounds.

00:01:58.409 --> 00:02:00.203
At the end of the day,
we're all Americans,

00:02:00.203 --> 00:02:02.372
and we will have to share
this country that we live in.

00:02:02.372 --> 00:02:04.541
What sort of change do you
want to see in the future?

00:02:04.541 --> 00:02:07.919
It is up to us as a nation
to engage civically,

00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:10.046
beyond just going
out to the polls.

00:02:10.046 --> 00:02:13.967
We can't stand on the sidelines
and expect these problems

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:15.718
in our society to
fix themselves.

00:02:15.718 --> 00:02:17.095
You can bring people together.

00:02:17.095 --> 00:02:18.721
You can bring together
those two sides,

00:02:18.721 --> 00:02:20.849
have those conversations,
reach across the aisle,

00:02:20.849 --> 00:02:21.683
shake that hand.

00:02:21.683 --> 00:02:23.852
Maybe our generation
could be the ones

00:02:23.852 --> 00:02:27.772
to end that gridlock
of partisanship.

00:02:27.772 --> 00:02:34.362
I'm optimistic, because so are
360 million other Americans.

00:02:34.362 --> 00:02:36.239
But we do have an
opportunity to change

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:37.532
the direction of this country.

00:02:37.532 --> 00:02:39.951
We need to all work together.

00:02:39.951 --> 00:02:41.244
It's critically important.

00:02:41.244 --> 00:02:43.788
Democracy is not
a spectator sport.

00:02:43.788 --> 00:02:45.123
Got to get off the sidelines.

00:02:45.123 --> 00:02:47.458
Democracy is not to
be taken for granted.

00:02:47.458 --> 00:02:48.918
Democracy is fragile.

00:02:48.918 --> 00:02:50.336
Something that has
to be defended,

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:51.754
preserved, and protected.

00:02:51.754 --> 00:02:53.673
It is up to us to keep it.

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:56.426
Young people, now more than
ever, involved in politics,

00:02:56.426 --> 00:02:57.177
and interested.

00:02:57.177 --> 00:02:58.678
And hoping that
this film kind of

00:02:58.678 --> 00:03:01.222
motivates them to
keep pushing, and push

00:03:01.222 --> 00:03:02.223
for fighting the good fight.

